# Nuclei

**Nuclei** by [ProjectDiscovery](https://projectdiscovery.io/) is an open-source, template-based vulnerability scanner. It is widely used by bug bounty hunters, security professionals, and engineering teams to automate vulnerability detection, misconfiguration checks, and security regressions across applications, cloud infrastructure, and networks.

How it works: Nuclei uses a vast library of simple YAML-based templates (similar to Semgrep's rules). These templates define the exact requests to be made (HTTP, DNS, TCP, JavaScript, etc.) and specify conditions (matchers and extractors) to confirm the vulnerability.

## Installing Nuclei locally (optional)

Before we run it as a GitHub Action, it's a good idea to practice using it locally, with on-demand scans.

You can find installation instructions [here](https://docs.projectdiscovery.io/opensource/nuclei/install), in summary here are some options:

- Go

```
go install -v github.com/projectdiscovery/nuclei/v3/cmd/nuclei@latest
```

- Brew

```
brew install nuclei
```

- Docker
  
```
docker pull projectdiscovery/nuclei:latest
```

## Running Nuclei locally (optional)

Let's test it against our deployed test instance for Jester Social at https://jester.social (assuming it is still up and running, which it may not be... in which case you can pick a different target!).

The test command we're running is:

```
nuclei -id tls-version -id http-missing-security-headers -u jester.social
```

Notice it only enables two specific checks: `tls-version` and `http-missing-security-headers`, which makes the scan run faster.

You could also enable another check by adding `-id rails-debug-mode`, which should return a result, since we have (intentionally) deployed this Rails instance in debug mode, which enables verbose error messages.

> [!Note]
> Nuclei has a large set of community-submitted templates. Consider which templates you want to enable by exploring the list here: https://github.com/projectdiscovery/nuclei-templates

